00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  i just got back from the canned film  festival which is arguably the biggest  most influential film festival in the  world hundreds of thousands of people  descend on the french riviera and throw  the glitziest most over-the-top  celebration of cinema  it's truly a place and an experience  like none other i've ever had and as i  emerge out of the fog of espresso jet  lag and amazing movies i want to talk  about what i watched there and my  experience at the festival this video is  sponsored by mubi  my first day at the festival involved  recovering from not getting any sleep on  the plane exploring the area and  watching the new david cronenberg film  in crimes of the future viggo mortensen  and leah sedough play artists where the  loss of ability to feel pain has caused  humanity to start experimenting with all  kinds of weird and grotesque surgeries  as a form of artistic expression it's a  lot of what you'd expect from cronenberg  i think the most interesting thing is  cronenberg is kind of setting up a world  where there's these characters that feel  like the changes that are happening in  their world are very scary and kind of  strike at the core of what they consider  to be human but meanwhile the entire  environment that they live in seems just  like completely inhuman and grotesque to  like our sensibility so there's an  interesting contrast there i like the  performances especially the one from  viggo mortensen uh and i think the world  building is interesting but ultimately  like i left a little bit underwhelmed  this was a great way to start the  festival but in hindsight since this is  coming out in the u.s this weekend  anyway i might have skipped this one and  watched something else while i was there  part of the fun of being at can is  you're in this curated environment and  so you can just walk into something at  random and it there stands a decent  chance that it might be  incredible so while there was a lot in  the fest that i was excited about coming  from directors that i was already  familiar with i wanted to make sure i  walked into at least a few things that i  knew nothing about and i spent my second  day at the festival doing entirely just  that the first thing i went to was a  greek comedy called dodo in this movie  there's a rich family and their staff  and friends and they're kind of  preparing for a wedding  and  having a weird like existential crisis  at the same time  and they find a dodo uh one of the  extinct birds on their property and it's  a weird ensemble comedy the tone is very  strange i think the presence of the dodo  bird is an interesting conceit because  it kind of plants this question in your  mind about  you know extinction or things dying out  and you're kind of wondering the whole  time who or what does the dodo represent  within the dynamic of this family but  the comedy was pretty much lost on me  and i don't think i was alone there were  not a lot of laughs in the screening i  was in i'm a big fan of a lot of the  greek cinema that i've seen but  unfortunately this one for me was just  kind of a miss next up i walked into a  film called pacifiction and i'm sure  this might be an interesting movie but  it's snail's pace kind of exploration of  neo capitalist ennui in the pacific was  really no match for my breakfast and  lunch skipping self at that point so  after an hour of just like confusion  with still another hour and 45 minutes  to go i decided to bounce and look for  some food i would have stuck it out and  i might try to rewatch this later when  it's available  but i would definitely was not the first  person to walk out of this screening so  i don't think it was really landing with  a decent amount of the audience after  stabilizing my blood sugar i walked into  butterfly vision and i had a bit more  success with this one this is a  ukrainian film about a prisoner of war  who is released back to her home and  just kind of the process she's going  through of grappling with the trauma of  her experience and reintegrating kind of  back into civilian society  this is a really heavy film dealing with  a lot of like intense issues that are  very topical and relevant right now to  things that are going on in ukraine i  think the most interesting thing about  this movie was the way they're using  kind of digital  uh artifacts and different forms of  video footage in a very kind of  expressionistic way and i think the lead  performance was very compelling there  was some of the cultural stuff that was  just a bit like over my head some of the  nuance i  am not familiar enough with what's going  on in that region to i think pick up on  all of the nuance of this film i don't  expect this one to make big waves in the  us this year but this was definitely an  impressive debut feature and i'll be  keeping an eye out for more from this  director in the future the last thing of  my second day was close by lucas dawn i  got to go to the gala screening of this  which meant black tie formal wear  walking up the red carpet the filmmakers  being present that was a pretty cool  experience the grand theater lumiere  where they show these premieres is  absolutely massive they pack it full of  people who are really excited to watch  these movies close is a an incredibly  gentle  heartfelt but like brutal gut-wrenching  film it kind of lures you in with this  nice  vibe and then just  hits you over the head with this like  intensity the performances from the two  boys in this film are absolutely  incredible this was one of my favorite  things i watched at the festival this  was what i expected to win the palm dior  it ended up tying for second place  getting the grand prix a24 picked this  up for the us distribution i expect this  to be a big film and probably a favorite  of the year for a decent amount of  people shout out to ethan one of my  viewers who gave me a ticket for this  premiere day three started out with the  new claire denis which is actually the  film that ended up tying with close for  the grand prix this movie is a very  strange joint margaret quali plays this  like drifter journalist who's stuck in  central america and she's just kind of  all over the place like looking for a  way to get back to the us and like her  character the plot of this film is just  like completely manic and erratic and  like all over the place and always going  in weird directions that you're not at  all expecting but it has a very specific  like vibe that i think is interesting  and the way claire denis uses like  setting and location as a backdrop for  the story i think is really interesting  like  her films are really  i get this feeling often about the place  and the people  in  the place is just kind of like  a way of like attracting our eyes  while she like sets up different issues  in the background which i think is  fascinating this definitely wasn't my  favorite thing at the festival but it  was getting a lot of hate from certain  critics and i don't think it deserved  that hate my main takeaway from this  movie is that margaret quality has  quite a career ahead of her if she keeps  up this kind of caliber of work and i'm  really excited to see how that unfolds  then it was one of my most anticipated  films of the festival the new film from  kelly reichardt showing up i really  loved first cow and so i was excited to  see this and fortunately i got to go to  the premiere which was really cool this  movie is  what i've come to expect from kelly  reichardt which is a very sort of gentle  anti-drama but that has like more depth  to it than maybe what meets the eye at  first glance it's about an artist in the  portland art scene and her  landlord and her family and her  co-workers taking care of a pigeon  doing art and it reminds me a lot of jim  jarmisch's patterson just in that it's  very like  calm and mellow  it's about art it's not a hugely  ambitious film but i think it's filling  its shoes perfectly and like being what  it needs to be i don't expect this to be  like a massive hit but if you're a fan  of kelly reichardt it's worth watching  the performance from michelle williams  is amazing she really kind of disappears  into this role  and i can't wait to just re-watch this  one periodically  day four started with probably the most  hyped film at the festival i've been  hearing about it nonstop since i showed  up and this was triangle of sadness from  ruben austland it's a satirical  comedy  like a farce about wealthy society class  social roles it's incredibly irreverent  over-the-top ridiculous it's also one of  the funniest things that i've watched in  a long time there's a set piece in the  middle of this film that is just  bonkers insane one of the craziest  things like i've seen in a comedy film  ever i think white lotus packed into  like a more extreme two and a half hour  film where woody harrelson plays a  marxist  ship captain  i can't wait to see  what people think of this film once it's  out in the u.s and i can't wait to watch  it again i think it's auslan's most  accessible film and maybe his best to  date last thing i got to see was another  very anticipated movie the new park  chanwook decision to leave my friend  julian who was with me at the festival  saw this the first day and was blown  away by it so i was very excited to see  it and this is an amazing film like park  chanwook's direction what he's doing  with composition and editing in this is  really incredible there's like  places he's putting the camera in this  that i've never seen  anywhere else before  i had a little bit of a hard time like  keeping up with the pace of it and all  the details because i was just so  exhausted at this point so i can't wait  to like  re-watch it look at it a few times  really kind of try to break down and  understand what's going on in this film  because it's a really well-oiled machine  i think this one's going to be a big hit  and mubi picked it up  for release so hopefully it'll be out  soon on mubi that's what i got to see  there was definitely a lot that i was  still interested in that i didn't get a  chance to see that i'll be looking  forward to catching up on once it's out  but i'm happy with what i got to see and  i think triangles of sadness decision to  leave and close will probably rank  pretty high on my favorite films of this  year and aside from getting to watch  some cool movies just being in this kind  of environment was pretty cool  it's kind of insane to experience  watching these kinds of movies with  people who are arguably maybe even more  excited about them than me when what i'm  used to in the us is like going to these  movies on opening night  in the midst of like a semi-empty  theater so just being in that  environment that's like celebrating film  in such an intense and crazy way it was  definitely a nice like counterpoint to  this feeling that kind of cinema is this  dying art form it's hard to feel that  way when you're in an environment that  is this excited about it but can is also  a place of like weird contradiction  you're surrounded by five-star hotels  expensive restaurants people in like  expensive tuxedos and dresses walking up  a red carpet and across the bay there's  like luxury yachts and meanwhile like  the movies you're watching are generally  very like humanitarian in focus  anti-colonialist the movie that won the  palm dior this year is just kind of like  a send-up of the entire like elitist  culture that kind of surrounds can the  city and the festival so i found being  in that kind of environment like almost  a very conflicted one i want to read  something that actor vincent linden who  was the president of the jury this year  at cannes said in his opening speech  although when we are crushed under the  weight of current affairs and i find  myself discouraged i do sometimes wonder  if we are not currently dancing on the  titanic and as i was sitting in like  really nice theaters along the french  riviera like watching incredible movies  in this like lavish environment and  hearing about horrific events that were  happening back in the us or like  watching movies about the conflict in  ukraine that was happening like not that  far from where i was sitting i  definitely felt that tension and those  words were kind of like present  in my mind the entire time and i  definitely felt this hopefulness that  like film can be this really meaningful  medium that can inspire change or like  have an impact on the world but then  there's also this tension that maybe in  part it's just another thing that's  distracting from the ship taking on  water in terms of which of those things  it is i don't think i know the answer  but  i think i share lyndon's hopefulness  that film can aspire to something more  and can achieve some kind of  authenticity that if we're going to make  films we might as well aspire to those  heights  he also said in his speech the time of  artists and responsible filmmakers has  come to bear us up and nourish our  imaginations to help us repeat to  ourselves at every opportunity in honor  of all those who are suffering and  fighting in this world be alive and be  aware of it festivals i can in a sense i  think are important and that they give  film something to aspire towards in  terms of artistry in terms of like  meaning that's beyond just you know box  office numbers commercialism for better  or for worse ken is a place where they  treat movies as like a legitimate form  of artistic expression that can really  say something and have an impact on the  world having a place that celeb tries to  celebrate  film in a more meaningful way hopefully  serves as a way to inspire the form to  be the best that it can be one of the  cool things about going to a festival as  i mentioned before is the curation of it  it's fun to just walk into stuff and  hope that you're gonna see something  really amazing and know that like  somebody chose that film or group of  people chose that film for a specific  reason to be a part of the festival you  can get that same kind of experience  with mubi my sponsor for this video mubi  is a hand curated online streaming  cinema they have a whole section called  the can takeover every year while can is  happening with films from past cannes  festivals that you can watch right now  as a part of the can takeover you can  watch austlin's last  film the square that also won the palm  dior in preparation for seeing triangle  of sadness this year that's available  right now on mubi in the us and they  bought some films from this year's can  most notably decision to leave the new  park chan wuk that will be coming up on  mubi eventually i don't know when yet  but mubi is a great way to explore films  in this kind of way get the sort of  festival experience without having to  get accredited for a festival and fly  all the way to france so i definitely  recommend you check movie out today you  can get an extended 30-day free trial  when you go to mubi.com thomasflight  click the link in the description or on  the screen that's  mubi.com  thomasflight  [Music]  [Music]  you

